even though westerns used to be  extremely popular stories of masked  horsemen robbing steam locomotives and  showdowns at high noon hardly ever make  their way into theaters these days let's  take a look at the real reasons why  Studios aren't making many westerns  anymore  the early 2010 saw a few attempts by  Hollywood to strike gold with big-budget  westerns and in most cases the studio's  found nothing but disappointment the  first of what became something of an  unholy trinity of Western killers was  2010's Jonah Hex starring Josh Brolin is  the anti-hero gunslinger based on the  popular DC Comics character Jonah Hex  was a massive failure despite Brolin  sharing the screen with other popular  actors like Megan Fox and Michael  Fassbender the film only earned a  quarter of the 47 million dollars it  cost to produce next came 2011 s Cowboys  &amp; Aliens which tried to weave a classic  alien invasion story into an Old West  backdrop the film was directed by Jon  Favreau who was coming off the hit Iron  Man 2 and boasted extremely bankable  stars such as Harrison Ford and Daniel  Craig unfortunately the movie fizzled at  the box office stateside and barely  broke even once it went overseas but the  crown jewel of Western disappointments  was 2013's The Lone Ranger one of the  most expensive flops ever made the film  starred army hammer is the masked hero  and Johnny Depp is is comrade Tonto  regardless of the star power and being  helped by blockbuster director Gore for  Vince key the movie was a huge bomb like  Cowboys and Aliens it needed to go  overseas to squeeze out a profit  originally The Lone Ranger was expected  to have at least one sequel but after  the film tanked studio execs were all  basically thinking the same thing don't  ever do that again  you can make a low-budget Western but if  you're looking for the kind of movie  that's going to compete with other big  action features it'll be pretty obvious  if you do it on the cheap she  and kill your people and your sheriff's  maybe  although it seems like westerns would be  affordable to produce with their simple  one Street towns and disheveled  characters wearing soiled clothing the  truth is that any authentic period piece  is going to come with a heavy price tag  a perfect example of this is the 2007  remake of 3:10 to Yuma starring Russell  Crowe and Christian Bale a new town was  built from scratch with a film but the  production ran out of money and so the  fact that the town was unfinished for  lack of funds was written into the story  and by the way despite featuring a list  actors like Crowe and Bale and earning  largely favorable reviews 3:10 to Yuma  barely turned a profit for some time  it's been predicted that China would  eventually surpass the u.s. and Canada  to become the biggest market in the  world for movie ticket sales this isn't  good news for westerns in the wake of  The Lone Ranger  Michael aggressed of the Atlantic took a  long look at why the genre was declining  among the many reasons agresa pointed  out exacerbating the problem is the  rejection of cowboy movies by  international audiences particularly the  Chinese it's another reason why the Lone  Ranger will probably be the last attempt  to build a true summer tentpole in the  genre considering how important China is  becoming to anyone making big-budget  films this could be the worst time in  Hollywood history to produce an epic  Western could be worse worse how could  this be worse think of westerns and  you'll inevitably conjure memories of  cowboy swapping stories and eating  around a giant campfire or traveling  across gorgeous landscapes on horseback  westerns can have plenty of long  drawn-out scenes that gradually build  the narrative and that's one of the  great things about the genre but when  you compare that style to something like  an Avengers movie where heroes are  blasting through cities and battling  armies of villains every other scene  westerns might seem a bit slow to some  modern moviegoers take a film like the  oscar-winning Unforgiven a practically  flawless movie it's still a beloved  Western decades after its release but  it's over two hours long and there isn't  a bonafide shootout until the final  moments of the film if you love westerns  then you probably appreciate the pacing  but if you're conditioned and non-stop  action you might feel like you deserve  more for the price of your ticket  deserves getting nothing to do with it  well the greatest westerns ever made  never relied on cross genre gimmicks or  special effects plenty of filmmakers had  tried to spice up their westerns with  modern sci-fi thrills  unfortunately it usually ends in  disaster one of the most recent examples  is Cowboys and Aliens which was a major  disappointment critically and  financially an older example is 1999's  wild wild west which fused a Western  with steampunk technology in the film  Kevin Klein's Artemus Gordon boasted an  arsenal of bizarre and occasionally  helpful inventions while the villain dr.  Arliss loveless played by Kenneth  Branagh was part man and part  steam-powered spider although it was  directed by hitmaker barry sonnenfeld  and starred Will Smith Wild Wild West  was quite deservedly a tremendous flop  that ended Smith's hot streak at the box  office one of the few exceptions to the  rule is Back to the Future Part 3 but it  was the third in a series of hugely  popular films that had nothing to do  with the frontier until its final  installment nevertheless the fact that a  time-traveling Western was a huge  blockbuster is still mind-boggling to  some crisps with westerns largely  underperforming against other big-budget  flicks movie studios are often in the  position of winning critical acclaim or  making a profit and for many the choice  between those two is clear take for  example the assassination of Jesse James  by the Coward Robert Ford while it  received largely positive reviews and  starred Brad Pitt's and Casey Affleck it  struggled to make back even half of its  30 million dollar budget more recently  there was the sisters brothers with john  c reilly and joaquin phoenix this widely  praised Western about a pair of unlikely  guns for hire made a mere 13 million  dollars worldwide and only three million  dollars domestically in many cases it  seems that today's best and most  imaginative westerns are being created  by independent filmmakers so the future  of this classic American genre might be  in art houses and not at your mainstream  Cineplex but if that means more  high-quality westerns on the big screen  we're sure fans won't mind one bit check  out one of our newest videos right here  plus even more looper videos about your  favorite movie genres are coming soon  subscribe to our YouTube channel and hit  the bell so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

